## Stone Selection for Rose Gold Emerald Cut Rings

The stone for an emerald cut rose gold ring follows the same proportion standards as any emerald cut: L:W 1.30–1.50, depth 60–64%, table 61–69%. Rose gold changes one buying decision: color grade.

**Color: Start at H, not G.** In rose gold, H-color emerald cut diamonds look identical to G in the same setting. The metal's warm pink tone absorbs the slight warmth of an H-grade stone. Buyers in white gold or platinum need G minimum. Buyers in rose gold can confidently start at H and save 15–25% on stone cost. [Browse H-color certified emerald cuts](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&colorMin=H&colorMax=H&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — compare prices to G before deciding.

**Clarity: VS2 minimum.** Rose gold changes nothing about clarity requirements. The emerald cut's step-cut table shows inclusions the same way regardless of metal. VS2 is the minimum for most buyers. VS1 is the safer recommendation for stones above 1.5ct where the table is large enough to make SI1 inclusions clearly visible.

**Carat: 1ct+ recommended.** The emerald cut needs sufficient face-up area to produce its characteristic hall-of-mirrors reflection pattern. Below 1ct, the step-cut facets are too small for the full effect. At 1ct (approximately 6.5mm × 4.5mm), the stone fills a rose gold solitaire band comfortably. Check the [complete emerald cut size guide](/emerald-cut-diamond-size-chart) for mm dimensions by carat weight.

**Natural 1ct (H-VS2) in rose gold:** [Browse certified H-VS2 emerald cuts](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&colorMin=H&colorMax=H&clarityMin=VS2&clarityMax=VS2&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — typically $2,400–$2,800 for a 1ct GIA H-VS2.

**Lab 1ct (F-VVS1) in rose gold:** [Browse lab IGI emerald cuts](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — approximately $450–$600 for a 1ct IGI F-VVS1.

